finish('optimal-explicit-read-once-branching-program-prg',
 id='TCS-6600', title='Optimal explicit pseudorandom generators for read-once branching programs',
 area='Pseudorandomness and derandomization', criterion='construction', question_type='yes_no',
 formal='Do there exist absolute constants C,D and a uniform family of generators G_{n,w,p}:{0,1}^s→{0,1}^n, for all integers n,w≥2 and p≥1, with s≤C(log_2 n+log_2 w+p), such that |Pr_{u uniform in {0,1}^s}[B(G_{n,w,p}(u))=1]−Pr_{z uniform in {0,1}^n}[B(z)=1]|≤2^(−p) for every length-n, width-at-most-w ordered read-once branching program B? Require a single deterministic algorithm that, given n,w,p,u and an output position i, computes G_{n,w,p}(u)_i using at most D(log_2 n+log_2 w+p) work bits and time (nw·2^p)^D.',
 definitions='An ordered read-once branching program has layers V_0,…,V_n, each containing at most w states, a designated initial state in V_0, and an accepting subset of V_n. For each layer i−1, each state has one outgoing edge labeled 0 and one labeled 1 to V_i. On input z, the program follows the edge labeled z_i at step i and accepts exactly when the final state is accepting. Different edges may merge; neither regularity nor permutation transitions are required. The order is the fixed order z_1,…,z_n. Programs may be arbitrary nonuniform objects, but the generator cannot inspect their descriptions. The parameters and seed are read-only inputs to the generator algorithm, and the output bit is written separately; all work space, counters and intermediate storage are counted. Seed length is a computable function of the parameters within the stated resources. Uniform seeds induce an ordinary probability distribution, without signed or externally assigned weights. Set ε=2^(−p); this dyadic formulation gives the usual O(log(nw/ε)) target for arbitrary positive error by rounding down to a smaller dyadic tolerance.',
 answer_criterion='Construct the uniform family with both the seed-length and workspace bounds and prove simultaneous fooling of all specified programs, or prove that no such family exists. Nonconstructive small sample spaces do not meet explicitness. A generator for only constant width, regular or permutation programs, a hitting set, or a signed weighted generator does not by itself settle the full formulation. A conditional construction must identify its unproved assumption.',
 context=[
  paragraph('The program processes a long stream while remembering only its current state. Width w corresponds to about log_2 w bits of memory; length n is the number of random bits it can inspect. A pseudorandom generator replaces that long random stream with the deterministic expansion of a much shorter genuinely random seed. The requirement is simultaneous: the same generated distribution must fool every permitted program, even though their transition tables may be entirely different.','nisan'),
  paragraph('There is no information-theoretic obstacle to the seed-length target. After padding to w states, the number of programs is at most w·2^w·w^(2nw). Choose a list of independent uniform n-bit strings and use a uniformly chosen list position as the seed. A concentration bound and union bound show that a list of O(ε^(−2)(nw log w+w)) strings suffices to approximate every acceptance probability. Indexing that list takes O(log n+log w+log(1/ε)) bits. The argument does not tell us how to generate its entries in the tiny workspace required here.'),
  paragraph('The computational payoff is direct. Fix an input to a bounded-error logarithmic-space randomized machine and view its evolution on random bits as a polynomial-length, polynomial-width branching program. A logarithmic seed permits enumeration of all seeds, regeneration of individual pseudorandom bits and counting accepting runs within logarithmic space. With constant error small enough to preserve the acceptance gap, this would prove L=BPL. The card asks for a uniform generator, which is a particular strong route to that complexity-class equality.','error2026'),
  paragraph('Nisan’s construction gives seed length O(log n·log(nw/ε)). For polynomial width and constant error this is O(log²n), so reaching the target would remove an entire logarithmic factor. The difficulty is generating a universally useful sample space in little space, rather than merely showing that some sample space exists. Even considerable improvements for simpler transition structures need not survive arbitrary merging of states.','nisan'),
  paragraph('Weighted pseudorandom generators relax the distribution requirement. They approximate acceptance by a weighted sum of program evaluations, with weights that may be negative. Hoza’s RANDOM 2021 construction attains seed length O(log²n+log(1/ε)) for width-n, length-n programs, improving the dependence on small error. Such weighted estimates can support deterministic simulation, but negative mass cannot be sampled as an ordinary probability distribution over the generated strings.','hoza'),
  paragraph('Cheng and Wu’s SODA 2026 work improves weighted generators across width and error regimes. Chen, Cohen, Doron, Khaskelberg and Ta-Shma’s May 2026 revision improves error reduction and the dependence on the input alphabet. It still constructs a weighted object. Its discussion credits Cheng and Hoza’s earlier reduction showing that sufficiently good ordinary-generator dependence on alphabet size and error would break the log-squared barrier. That implication does not turn the newly constructed weighted generator into an ordinary one.','error2026'),
  paragraph('The role of hitting sets provides another useful distinction. A hitting set ensures that an accepting string is included when the accepting fraction is sufficiently large; it does not directly approximate that fraction. Cheng and Hoza prove that sufficiently strong logspace hitting sets nevertheless imply two-sided logspace derandomization through a more involved reduction. Their results explain why a derandomization theorem need not arrive packaged as the universal probability distribution requested here.','hitting'),
  paragraph('Cohen, Doron and Goldgraber’s July 2026 paper gives an ordinary generator for permutation programs with seed O((log w+log(1/ε))log n), by a new analysis of the INW generator. In this subclass each symbol acts bijectively on the states between layers. The result has optimal logarithmic dependence on length when width and error are fixed, but the permutation promise excludes the arbitrary state merging allowed in this card.','permutation')],
 why='An optimal generator would remove randomness from logarithmic-space computation and identify how little entropy suffices to fool every small-memory streaming computation. The question joins explicit constructions, space complexity and the gap between real distributions and useful signed approximations.',
 importance=dict(score=97, method='editorial', assessed_on='2026-09-10', reason='A central constructive route to L=BPL, with optimal nonconstructive seed length known and a persistent gap for uniform space-efficient generators despite advances in restricted and weighted models.'),
 progress=[
  step('1992','Nisan constructs generators using O(log n·log(nw/ε)) seed bits for general ordered read-once branching programs.','nisan'),
  step('2021','Hoza obtains weighted generators with additive log(1/ε) dependence and notes deterministic simulation in space O(S^(3/2)/sqrt(log S)); this is not a logarithmic-seed ordinary PRG.','hoza'),
  step('2020–2022','Cheng and Hoza show that suitable logspace hitting sets imply L=BPL and analyze stronger reductions toward ordinary PRGs; the journal version appears on 20 September 2022.','hitting'),
  step('2026-01-07','Cheng and Wu publish improved weighted PRGs via weighted pseudorandom reductions.','weighted2026'),
  step('2026-05-04','Chen and coauthors post revision 3 of Improved Error Reduction for Weighted PRGs. The revision preserves the weighted-output distinction and attributes the alphabet/error implication to Cheng and Hoza.','error2026'),
  step('2026-07-21','Cohen, Doron and Goldgraber give an improved INW analysis for permutation programs. Arbitrary ordered programs remain outside its structural promise.','permutation')],
 references=[
  ref('nisan','Pseudorandom generators for space-bounded computation','Noam Nisan',1992,'https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~sbuss/CourseWeb/Math268_2013W/Nisan_PRG.pdf','Combinatorica 12(4):449–461, DOI 10.1007/BF01305237; original generator construction'),
  ref('hoza','Better Pseudodistributions and Derandomization for Space-Bounded Computation','William M. Hoza',2021,'https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.APPROX/RANDOM.2021.28','RANDOM 2021, 28:1–28:23; weighted generators and improvement to Saks–Zhou simulation'),
  ref('hitting','Hitting Sets Give Two-Sided Derandomization of Small Space','Kuan Cheng; William M. Hoza',2022,'https://theoryofcomputing.org/articles/v018a021/','Theory of Computing 18(21):1–32, 20 September; CCC 2020 precursor; hitting sets, PRGs and alphabet dependence'),
  ref('weighted2026','Weighted Pseudorandom Generators for Read-Once Branching Programs via Weighted Pseudorandom Reductions','Kuan Cheng; Ruiyang Wu',2026,'https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/1.9781611978971.124','SODA 2026, pp. 3423–3458, 7 January; weighted constructions across parameter regimes'),
  ref('error2026','Improved Error Reduction for Weighted PRGs','Ben Chen; Gil Cohen; Dean Doron; Yuval Khaskelberg; Amnon Ta-Shma',2026,'https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/064/','ECCC TR26-064, revision 3, 4 May; §1 general PRG frontier, Theorem 1.1 and Corollary 1.2 weighted construction, §5 ordinary-PRG implication; original April title was Toward Improving Nisan’s PRG via Deweightization'),
  ref('permutation','A Forward-Backward Weight Analysis of INW for Permutation Branching Programs','Gil Cohen; Dean Doron; Noam Goldgraber',2026,'https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2026/123/','ECCC TR26-123, 21 July; ordinary INW generator under permutation transitions, not a theorem for general merging programs')],
 status='source_open', status_note='Checked through 10 September 2026. ECCC TR26-064 revision 3 explicitly retains the optimal general PRG target and the log-squared barrier. Its new generator is weighted; the July TR26-123 result is restricted to permutation programs. This card requires one ordinary, program-independent distribution family and optimal seed length as well as optimal work space.', formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-10')
